WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.160
Threat hunting depends on available, trustworthy telemetry.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.105
A hunter can only reason from evidence that exists, is accessible,

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:07.775
and can be interpreted correctly.

00:00:07.775 --> 00:00:11.070
The best hypothesis will fail if the required data was never collected,

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:15.360
is retained for too short a period, or lacks the fields needed to identify users, hosts,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.840
commands, or destinations.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.840
This is why telemetry review is part of the hunt, not a separate administrative task.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.470
Common data sources include endpoint process events, authentication logs, DNS logs,

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:30.560
proxy and firewall logs, EDR telemetry, cloud control-plane logs, identity-provider logs,

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.890
and email security logs.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.805
Endpoint process events help answer questions about parent-child process chains,

00:00:35.805 --> 00:00:38.765
command lines, file execution, and persistence behavior.

00:00:38.765 --> 00:00:42.715
Authentication logs help answer questions about account use, source locations,

00:00:42.715 --> 00:00:45.400
device changes, and failed or successful access.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:49.600
DNS and proxy logs help answer questions about domains and outbound destinations.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.930
Cloud control-plane logs show administrative actions such as role changes, key creation,

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.120
storage access, or unusual API calls.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:00.375
Data quality matters as much as data presence. Missing fields can make a useful query

00:01:00.375 --> 00:01:03.150
impossible. Clock drift can make a timeline look wrong.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.600
Inconsistent hostnames can split one asset into several identities. NAT can cause many

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.190
systems to share one source IP.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:13.579
Short log retention can hide the start of an activity chain. Noisy event volume can bury

00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:15.675
relevant events under routine software behavior.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:19.720
A hunt should account for these issues instead of pretending the data is perfect. Trust

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.320
also has layers. An endpoint agent may be offline.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.320
A cloud log may be delayed. A firewall log may show connection metadata without

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:27.475
application detail.

00:01:27.475 --> 00:01:31.440
An identity log may show a successful sign-in but not what the user did afterward.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.010
Hunters should understand what each source can prove and what it cannot prove.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.620
A proxy log may show that a system connected to a domain,

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.818
but it may not prove which process made the request unless endpoint data is correlated.

00:01:41.818 --> 00:01:43.790
Telemetry gaps are valid hunt findings.

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:48.060
If the hunt cannot answer a question because DNS logs are missing from a network segment

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.845
or cloud administrative logs are not retained long enough,

00:01:50.845 --> 00:01:51.630
document that gap.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.540
That outcome can drive logging improvements, retention changes, parsing fixes,

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:56.735
or new detection coverage.

00:01:56.735 --> 00:02:00.680
A beginner threat hunter should build a habit of asking three telemetry questions before

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:01.200
querying:

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.030
what source should show this behavior, what fields do I need,

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.370
and what limitations could weaken my conclusion?

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:10.320
That habit keeps the investigation grounded in evidence and prevents overconfidence.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.805
Telemetry review should also include access and privacy expectations.

00:02:13.805 --> 00:02:17.510
Hunters should use approved data sources, follow retention and handling rules,

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.425
and avoid collecting more information than the hunt requires.

00:02:20.425 --> 00:02:23.760
Good hunting respects both security needs and responsible data handling.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.840
The point is to answer the defensive question with enough evidence,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.440
not to expose unrelated user or business activity.
